then choose what they like and critics  like me can choose what they think is  artistically successful you you talk  about how South Park exemplifies a lot  of your theme  and you see traces of influences of some  great political philosophers amongst the  underpants gnomes in your chapter  Cartman shrugs can you talk about that a  little bit well South Park is a perfect  example of what I have in mind it's a  really interesting provocative  thoughtful show it's a very libertarian  show it defends capitalism defends  entrepreneurship it grows out of a long  tradition of philosophical humor the  main charge against South Park is that  it's dirty the language is filthy but I  point out in the chapter that you can  find that and Rabelais you can find it  in Shakespeare you can find it in  chaucer you can find it in Mark Twain uh  where are the kind of literary  antecedents or kind of what are the  roots of the wrap-up lesson at the end  of south park I think that's sort of an  artificial device that many people know  about the show is it something about  sort of authority has to be rational or  you have to kind of derive the lesson  yourself as a viewer or is it just a  cheesy TV trope that works I think it's  more of the latter but it's a long  tradition of works of literature of  drawing some kind of moral at the end  and they're having fun with that and  really point out to people that these  shows are not the mindless entertainment  many people claim they are and some of  those endings are in fact very  thoughtful very balanced judicious  resolutions of issues I show that for  example in the episode about gay Scout  leaders that on the one hand the show  makes fun of people who don't like  homosexuals and don't want them in the  Boy Scouts on the other hand they would  be homosexual Boy Scouts leader himself  defends freedom of association I'm proud  to be gay and I program free to express  all but freedom is a two-way street if  I'm free to express myself then the  scouts have to be free to express  themselves to himself feels the Scout  should be able to exclude him not  because he would do it himself but  because that's their right as an  organization that's a very balanced  account of a very hot issue and they do  it in episode that also features a  character  named Big Gail yes that's what we're  talking about here big gay al is out and  proud excellent uh so academic cultural  criticism kind of veers between two  extremes right you have your kind of  Frankfurt School inspired popular  culture is just here to anesthetize the  masses and make them amenable to  consumption and then you have I think  this sort of maybe it's a newer critique  that we're living in this very  fragmented media landscape and that the  internet and niche publishing make it so  that no one even has kind of a basis of  common conversation anymore I suspect  you would say that both of those schools  are wrong but can you kind of talk about  why well the Frankfurt School was a  product of German émigré is coming to  Hollywood in the 1940s and for example  they didn't find Donald Duck funny hi  later but not you staged by God if I  didn't speak English I might not find  Donald Duck funny one of my thesis about  the Frankfurt School he says the product  of Emma Gray's who really didn't  understand American culture they viewed  it the way an anthropologist would view  the strange customs of a foreign land I  think it's very sad that a whole strain  of pop culture criticism has grown out  of that as for the other view they're  missing the main point that in fact  right now television is forming our  common culture for good or ill that is  what I see among my students among my  academic friends we come to a conference  about Shakespeare at dinner we sit  around discussing Breaking Bad Mad Men  and the walking dead so your day job is  teaching Shakespeare and Homer to  college students on the side you publish  about Gilligan's Island and South Park  but you have said in the past that you  would never do zombies and now your  position on that is weakening what  happened yes I finally felt too much  pressure that I ought to see if my  thesis would apply to zombies so I've  just written an essay on the walking  dead and it led me to reflect on the  widespread apocalyptic scenarios  in American popular culture now zombie  plays alien invasion and I realized that  the primary fantasy in American  television right at the moment is to get  rid of the federal government after the  blackout nothing worked governments fell  militias throws up on the world we know  is gone but keeping our humanity that's  a choice we love situations where aliens  invade and destroy the federal  government zombies show up and destroy  the federal government and people want  to see what life would be like and of  course it's tough it's dangerous but  actually and all these shows a lot of  good things results families get back  together people start homeschooling  their children they have home health  care they become self-reliant the first  season of Walking Dead culminates in the  destruction of the Centers for Disease  Control in Atlanta and I can't think of  a better anti-federal fantasy than that  on that optimistic note thank you so  much for talking with us Paul I'm  speaking today with paul cantor author  of the invisible hand in popular culture  I'm Katherine manga award for reason TV  haha

